[
    {
        "content": "<p>I want to start a discussion about potentially changing the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bornology.IsVonNBounded#doc\">docs#Bornology.IsVonNBounded</a> (which should go out of the <code>Bornology</code> namespace, but that's another question).</p>\n<p>First, let me recall that this definition is originally meant to be used in the context of topological vector spaces (crucially, over a <strong><em>nontrivially normed field</em></strong>), where they generalize the bounded sets associated to a norm.</p>\n<p>Our current definition, matching Bourbaki, is the following: consider <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">K</span></span></span></span> a nontrivially normed field, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span> a TVS over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span>, and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S \\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8193em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊆</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span>. We say that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> is <em>bounded</em> if it is <em>absorbed</em> (<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Absorbs#doc\">docs#Absorbs</a>) by every neighborhood of zero. Unfolding the definition, this means that for every neighborhood <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 : E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span>, we have <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S \\subseteq kV</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8193em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊆</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> for every <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k : \\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">K</span></span></span></span> of large enough norm.</p>\n<p>Of course, in typical Mathlib fashion, we noticed that inversion did not play a role in this statement, so we generalized the definition to allow for any doc#SeminormedRing as scalars. </p>\n<p>Now, consider the following alternative definition: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> is <em>bounded (v2)</em> if, for every neighborhood <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 : E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span></span></span></span>, we have <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kS \\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8304em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊆</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span> for all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k : \\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">K</span></span></span></span> in a neighborhood of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0 : \\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">K</span></span></span></span>. This definition is of course equivalent over a field, because (excluding <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span> for which the inclusion is trivial), <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> going to zero is the same as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> going to infinity. Of course this breaks down terribly over a general ring.</p>\n<p>Yet, I claim this second formulation is the one which generalizes better to topological modules. I will list a few reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>you only need topologies on the module and the ring to state it.</li>\n<li>in fact, in their exercises, Bourbaki mentions this definition as the right one when the base filed is merely topological, not normed.</li>\n<li>even in the case of fields, I claim that this characterization is more natural. We typically use it in the form of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bornology.isVonNBounded_iff_tendsto_smallSets_nhds#doc\">docs#Bornology.isVonNBounded_iff_tendsto_smallSets_nhds</a>.</li>\n<li>this is a notion that shows up in very algebraic contexts, even for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E = \\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">E</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">K</span></span></span></span>. A recent example is <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40013\">#40013</a>, and I had also noticed this notion in <em>Topological Rings</em> by Kaplansky.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 598979177,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780231853
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Now, one reason I was hesitant to push this earlier is that I'm not sure what's the best way to proceed. In particular, I do not have an answer to the following question:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>should we just ditch our current notion of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bornology.IsVonNBounded#doc\">docs#Bornology.IsVonNBounded</a> ? I claim it is less useful, but I can think of contexts (e.g in geometric group theory) where it is indeed more natural to look at things going to infinity rather than things going to zero.</li>\n<li>if we have both notions (either as a temporary or definitive state), what should the names be?</li>\n<li>we could go even further and change our definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Absorbs#doc\">docs#Absorbs</a> to the following: we would say that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> absobs <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span></span></span></span> if we have <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kT \\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8304em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊆</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> for all scalars <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> in a neighborhood of zero. This changes the assumptions on the scalar type <code>M</code> from <code>[Bornology M]</code> to <code>[Zero M] [Topology M]</code>. Again, I think this is a good change, but I'm also worried about removing the existing definition.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 598979565,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780232233
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'd love to have input on this from <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"214703\">@Yury G. Kudryashov</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"412682\">@Moritz Doll</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"197836\">@Jireh Loreaux</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130609\">@Antoine Chambert-Loir</span></p>",
        "id": 598979628,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780232312
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>A few remarks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>About <code>VonN</code>. I would either remove it (<code>Bornology.IsBounded</code>) or write “VonNeumann” in full, by respect for this mathematician.</li>\n<li>This would give you <code>IsBounded</code>, or <code>Bornology.IsBounded</code> if you need to disambiguate from <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Filter.IsBounded#doc\">docs#Filter.IsBounded</a>.</li>\n<li>The given definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Absorbs#doc\">docs#Absorbs</a> is already a departure from Bourbaki (EVT, I, §1, n°5, def. 4), which does not use bornologies — they're defined later, and the move you propose brings you that definition. (Note how Bourbaki states it both ways)<br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1218x184\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/OkhWvzgC24v51lM8jmeicFmQ/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/OkhWvzgC24v51lM8jmeicFmQ/image.png/840x560.webp\"></li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 598981580,
        "sender_full_name": "Antoine Chambert-Loir",
        "timestamp": 1780234078
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(I suspect that their main choice is only due to the fact that there's no restriction on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">λ</span></span></span></span>, while they have to state that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu\\neq0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>, otherwise if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> absorbs <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span>, then <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\in A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6835em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>, whatever <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span>).</p>",
        "id": 598981711,
        "sender_full_name": "Antoine Chambert-Loir",
        "timestamp": 1780234211
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Bornology.IsBounded#doc\">docs#Bornology.IsBounded</a> already exists, so we do need a way to distinguish</p>",
        "id": 598981934,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780234424
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry. I will edit.</p>",
        "id": 598982085,
        "sender_full_name": "Antoine Chambert-Loir",
        "timestamp": 1780234578
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No worries !</p>",
        "id": 598982106,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780234597
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that Bourbaki's definition of bounded is simpler in this form, they say that there exists <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">λ</span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset \\lambda V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊂</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">λV</span></span></span></span>. Otherwise, they would have to say that there exists a <em>nonzero</em> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu A \\subset V</annotation></semantics></math></span><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"katex-html\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊂</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\">V</span></span></span></span>. <br>\n<img alt=\"image.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1296x284\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/3121/KIg2u5ZN5om9CZlFoOVnqKLC/image.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/3121/KIg2u5ZN5om9CZlFoOVnqKLC/image.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 598982177,
        "sender_full_name": "Antoine Chambert-Loir",
        "timestamp": 1780234655
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, but this characterization is again very specific to fields, otherwise one can't use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=nhds_basis_balanced#doc\">docs#nhds_basis_balanced</a></p>",
        "id": 598983666,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780235920
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The first quote from Bourbaki (about the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Absorbs#doc\">docs#Absorbs</a>) sent by Antoine suggests to me that we should move forward with the most aggressive change, namely changing the definition of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Absorbs#doc\">docs#Absorbs</a> directly (and then the definition of boundedness doesn't need to change at all). I'd still like more opinions. In particular, is anyone aware of a downstream project where this would cause disruptions?</p>",
        "id": 599451076,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780402025
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think changing absorbs makes the most sense. I think what we have in LeanOA is sufficiently separated from the underlying definition of von Neumann boundedness that we won't be affected, except maybe in one place.</p>",
        "id": 599523170,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1780410448
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I won't have time to start this refactor until next week; feel free to beat me to it.</p>",
        "id": 599835349,
        "sender_full_name": "Anatole Dedecker",
        "timestamp": 1780500409
    }
]